---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GuideRelated from '../../../components/GuideRelated.astro';
import { SITE_EN } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'Pixel Art Grid Snapping & Sprite Sheet Atlas Export — AIGC Studio';
const description =
  'Fix blurry edges, mixels, and noisy palettes offline on your machine: grid detection, Oklab color quantization, frame preview, and atlas.json export.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE_EN.name },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE_EN.name },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE_EN.domain}/en/guides/ai-pixel-art-sprite`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Do Pixel Snap and Sprite Slicer require a cloud GPU?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'No. Both Pixel Snap (edge-period grid detection + Oklab quantization) and Sprite Slicer run deterministically in your local browser canvas with zero VRAM and zero network calls.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Can exported sprite bundles go straight into Godot or Unity?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Yes. Clicking Apply writes spritesheet.png, individual frame PNGs, and atlas.json with frame, trimmed, and spriteSourceSize coordinates into a composite asset bundle.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/guides/ai-pixel-art-sprite" lang="en" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="section section-first">
    <div class="container article-wrap">
      <div class="section-idx">GUIDE / GAME ASSET</div>
      <h1>Pixel Art Grid Snapping &amp; Sprite Sheet Atlas Export</h1>
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>Audience: Indie game devs &amp; 2D artists</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        Diffusion models generate compelling “pixel-style” props and sprites at a glance, yet zooming in almost always reveals three production blockers: inconsistent pixel block widths (mixels like 5.3px or 6.7px), semi-transparent anti-aliased fringes, and hundreds of stray transition colors. That raw output cannot go straight into Godot, Unity, or Aseprite.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Deterministic local pipeline (0 GPU, 0 Python):</strong>
        Pixel Snap and Sprite Slicer in AIGC Studio’s Asset Workshop run locally in real time without spinning up a cloud GPU. Preview adjustments freely and commit to the version tree only when ready.
      </div>

      <h2>1. Why Naive Downscaling Fails</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bilinear scaling</strong> blurs crisp sprite silhouettes and introduces muddy alpha edges.</li>
        <li><strong>Nearest-neighbor scaling</strong> breaks whenever visual pixel widths are non-integer—producing uneven rows and broken single-pixel outlines.</li>
        <li><strong>sRGB color reduction</strong> crushes dark metallic and skin tones into flat muddy grays.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Three-Step Pixel Snap Pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>Workshop → Game &amp; 3D → Pixel Snap</strong>, select a generated prop or import a local draft, and the tool runs three deterministic stages:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Edge-period grid detection</strong>: Estimates the true underlying pixel step from horizontal/vertical gradient energy—or locks to an explicit target grid such as <code>16×16</code>, <code>32×32</code>, or <code>64×64</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Perceptual Oklab palette quantization</strong>: Applies median-cut and K-Means refinement in perceptually uniform Oklab space, collapsing stray noise into clean 8 / 16 / 32-color palettes.</li>
        <li><strong>Hard alpha binarization</strong>: Strips leftover semi-transparent fringe pixels so sprites sit cleanly on any background.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="table-wrap" style="margin: 26px 0 32px; padding: 20px;">
        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 18px; align-items: center;">
          <figure style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">
            <div class="ws-checker" style="padding: 20px; border: 1px solid var(--line);">
              <img src="/assets/workshop/pixel-before.webp" alt="Blurry pixel draft before snapping" style="width: 180px; height: 180px; margin: 0 auto; object-fit: contain;" />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="mono" style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--ink-3);">Before: Blurry anti-aliased input</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">
            <div class="ws-checker" style="padding: 20px; border: 1px solid var(--line);">
              <img src="/assets/workshop/pixel-after.webp" alt="Snapped pixel grid and Oklab palette result" style="width: 180px; height: 180px; margin: 0 auto; object-fit: contain; image-rendering: pixelated;" />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="mono" style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--ink-3);">After: Crisp grid + quantized palette</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>3. Sprite Sheet Slicing &amp; atlas.json Export</h2>
      <p>
        For multi-frame idle, walk, or attack sheets, strip the solid backdrop first with <strong>Remove Background</strong>, then open <strong>Sprite Slicer</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Overlay row/column slice guides directly on the canvas with adjustable padding and grid counts.</li>
        <li>Inspect real-time frame-by-frame animation playback at adjustable FPS to catch foot sliding or jitter.</li>
        <li>Click Apply to export a composite bundle inside the same version node containing <code>spritesheet.png</code>, individual frame PNGs, and <code>atlas.json</code> (<code>frame</code>, <code>trimmed</code>, and <code>spriteSourceSize</code> metadata).</li>
      </ul>

      <pre><code>{`outputs/20261124-120000-<jobId>/
├── 01.png              # Source image or master sheet
├── meta.json           # Lineage & slice parameters
├── spritesheet.png     # Grid-aligned sprite sheet
└── atlas.json          # Frame coordinates ready for game engines`}</code></pre>

      <h2>4. Practical Tips &amp; Boundaries</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cut out before quantizing</strong>: Running BiRefNet / RMBG-2.0 cutout before Pixel Snap prevents background colors from wasting slots in a 16-color palette.</li>
        <li><strong>Complex animation frames still benefit from touch-up</strong>: Multi-cell AI sheets can exhibit subtle outfit drift between frames; this workflow turns unusable blurry drafts into clean baselines ready for final polish in Aseprite or Godot.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Try the interactive before/after slider on the <a href="/en/workshop" class="link-inline">Asset Workshop page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <GuideRelated currentSlug="ai-pixel-art-sprite" lang="en" />
</BaseLayout>
